WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.876
Idea. Immunity means your body can destroy a pathogen before you become ill. It has two names. Active or passive: who made the antibodies? Natural or artificial: did it happen in normal life, or from a medical procedure?

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:34.763
Natural active: you catch the disease. Artificial active: you have a vaccine. Natural passive: a baby gets its mother's antibodies across the placenta, or in breast milk. Artificial passive: an injection of antibodies, or plasma from someone who has recovered.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:01:00.031
Method. Step one: ask who made the antibodies. If the person's own lymphocytes, the white blood cells that make antibodies, made them, it is active. It is slow to start, but memory cells make it last. Memory cells remember the antigen for years. If the antibodies were received ready-made, it is passive. It works at once, but no memory cells are made, so it does not last.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:12.411
Step two: ask how it happened. In normal life, it is natural. From a medical procedure, such as an injection, it is artificial. A vaccine is artificial and active.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:23.633
On a graph, passive immunity is high at once, then falls quickly as the antibodies are broken down. Active immunity rises slowly, then stays high for longer.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:42.182
Someone already ill needs antibodies now, so give passive immunity. Plasma from a recovered person has antibodies that bind to the virus antigens, so phagocytes destroy it. The plasma is tested first, and the red cells are removed so the blood does not clump.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:57.896
Worked example. Here is a real exam question. Two graphs show antibody levels after two types of injection. We will draw them next. Identify the form of artificial immunity each injection gives. Two marks.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:12.985
Both are injections, so both are artificial. Injection one: the level is high almost at once, and it falls to nothing by about forty days. Ready-made antibodies were injected. So it is artificial passive.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:29.277
Injection two: the level rises slowly to a peak at about thirty days, and stays up much longer. The body made its own antibodies. So it is artificial active. Answer by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:34.445
So the answer is one type per line, in the same order as the injections.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:46.983
Common mistake. A common mistake is to call a vaccine passive, because the person does nothing. A vaccine makes the body produce its own antibodies, so it is active.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:58.674
Another mistake is to call antibodies in breast milk artificial. Breast feeding is normal life, so it is natural passive. And passive immunity never lasts long.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:19.447
Your turn. Classify each one. One: a farmer catches measles from her child and recovers. Two: a man bitten by a snake is given antibodies against the venom. Three: a nurse has a hepatitis B vaccine. Then say which one works fastest. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:36.021
Answer. The farmer caught measles, so her own lymphocytes made antibodies, in normal life: natural active. The nurse's vaccine is artificial active. The snakebite antibodies were injected ready-made: artificial passive.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:47.255
The snakebite injection works fastest. The antibodies are ready-made, so they act at once. The other two need the body to make its own antibodies, which takes days.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:56.061
So, every time: who made the antibodies decides active or passive. How it happened decides natural or artificial.
